an  rights guarantees and the ability of of  people to to  hold the united states responsible for  damaging their lives and in many ways so  i think they're broad implications there  in the uk the chicanes have been much  more successful having won three major  lawsuits in 2000  2006 and 2007 in which  british courts the high court in london  has ruled their expulsion unlawful under  british law calling the expulsion  repugnant and an abuse of power  unfortunately last october the highest  court in the uk the house of lords  overturned those lower court rulings  by a three to two verdict ruling  that  the military and financial  considerations of the british government  could trump the jugosian's right of  abode and their right to return  which i think again essentially that the  ruling upheld colonial law the the right  of the british executive in the name of  the queen to make laws for  colonized people the chagosians part of  the british indian ocean territory um  they are now um appealing that ruling to  the european court of human rights where  they expect to have a um and hope to  have a better a better hearing  um and just last um  about six weeks ago in march  the european union passed a resolution  calling for the eu  to support the chigosians uh right to  return and and they're resettling their  their islands and in fact the jegos have  a an entire resettlement plan that i  could describe later  so what you see is growing international  momentum in support of the chagosians  right of return and their efforts to  return  in the uk and elsewhere in europe  in africa and increasingly here in the  u.s  um the chicago students are hoping to  begin  lobbying the obama administration and  hoping that the obama administration  will be  um  we'll have a again a more sympathetic  year and begin to accept the  responsibility  of the us government that successive  administrations have consistently denied  and in fact that's you know  my hope for the book is that it it